From 607aabf8041043a826502ef03cc2989cad67491d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Milan Patel <patelmm79@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 19:20:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fix(doctor): scope plugin npm-root scan to ledger-owned roots
 (#143) (#8)

* fix(doctor): scope plugin peer-link verification to plugins' own package dirs

The plugin-verification/peer-link phase walked every managed npm root,
including all accumulated artifact-generation trees
({package}__openclaw-generation__g-<hash>), each a full built npm tree
with its own node_modules. On a single-agent deploy this re-scan of the
whole captures root (~583MB after ~10 deploys) took 10-15 minutes and
tripped the deploy chain's 900s doctor cap.

Scope the peer-link audit/relink to the managed npm roots that own the
plugins' own package directories per the install ledger, so repeated
scans are O(active plugins) instead of O(all bytes under the captures
root). Fixes DarojaAI/openclaw-gateway #143.

* test(doctor): pin that a killed maintenance owner's lock is reclaimed

A doctor killed mid offline-SQLite-maintenance (deploy cap, interrupt, or
OOM/SIGKILL) leaves a sqlite-maintenance state lock owned by a now-dead
pid. The lock already stamps the owner pid + boot-time start identity and a
subsequent run reclaims a dead owner (gateway-lock stale-owner detection),
so a crashed run can never permanently wedge future maintenance. This
regression test pins that contract: a stale lock whose owner pid is no
longer alive is reclaimed by the next maintenance run, which proceeds and
releases the lock. Fixes DarojaAI/openclaw-gateway #142.

